  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA healthy dose of isentropic lift and moisture to the north of \na low pressure center over the NE/IA border continues to produce\nan impressive swath of rainfall over most of the WFO MPX \ncoverage area this morning. The low will shift north into \nsouthern MN and diminish, but not before rainfall totals in the \n0.50\"-1.00\" range have been realized from this system, owing to \nPWATs around 1.0\" nudging northward in tandem with the surface \nlow. Though the rainfall will end from southwest to northeast \nacross our coverage area through this afternoon, cloud cover \nwill remain as there is no dry air to aid in scouring out the \nlow-mid levels. On the contrary, moisture and temperatures will \ncontinue to slowly increase from today through Wednesday as the \nwashed-out surface low aids in frontogenesis across southern MN \nthis evening which will then rapidly shift north tonight into \nWednesday. This warm air advection is slightly evident this \nmorning as temperatures overnight near the IA border have \npunched into the lower 50s while temperatures over the vast \nmajority of the coverage area range from the upper 30s in \ncentral MN through the 40s in southern MN and western WI. The \nwarming trend will continue today as highs are expected to range\nfrom the upper 40s in west-central MN to the lower 60s near and\neast of the US-169 corridor. \n\nThis late October warm-up will continue in earnest on Wednesday,\nwhich is looking to be the warmest day in this forecast \nperiod. Highs will surge to the 60s area-wide, with some lower \n70s likely to be realized in southern MN into western WI. This \nwarmth will only last the one day as the cold front of this \nsynoptic system will push through the region Wednesday \nafternoon-evening. Pushing this front along will be a mid-level \ntrough over the north-central states, helping enhance mid-to-\nupper level SW flow across the region. As previously mentioned, \nalong with the WAA will be elevated moisture advection to the \ntune of dewpoints into the 50s to near 60, which could aid in \nproducing MLCAPEs of around 1000 j/kg Wednesday afternoon. With \nthe frontal passage in favorable afternoon-evening timing in \ntandem with peak heating/moisture, there is a possibility of \nisolated to scattered thunderstorm development in addition to \nfairly good rainfall coverage. COnfidence isn't overly \nimpressive to have strong storms, but the possibility is there \nfor some thunderstorms to be organized and produce small hail \nand/or gusty winds. \n\nThis cold front will push well into the Great Lakes by daybreak \nThursday with cooler high pressure filling in behind it. \nHowever, with its origin of a more Pacific nature than Canadian,\nthe airmass will only cool off slightly behind it with highs \ndropping to the mid 50s to mid 60s from Thursday through early \nnext week, which are values still above normal through the end \nof October into early November. Another round of rain looks \nlikely Friday into Saturday as isentropic lift is again \nheightened over southern portions of the coverage area in \nadvance of a warm front gaining impetus over the central Plains \ninto the mid-Mississippi River Valley. A similar situation to \nthis coming Wednesday then looks to potentially develop for \nearly next week, with the warm front snapping northward rapidly \nwith an approaching cold front from the west on Monday. We need \nto see if this situation develops because additional \nthunderstorms would be possible given the similar synoptic \nscenario.\n\nOne item to start thinking about is when we could start seeing \nfreezing temperatures return to our region. Long-range ensembles\nstill portray the thinking advertised in the previous \ndiscussion, that we may not see solid freezing temperatures \nuntil at least the middle of November.",
